Yes. In application security and bug bounty, you need to recognize the functions that can allow attacker-controlled input to reach operating-system command execution.

These functions are commonly associated with OS Command Injection and Remote Code Execution (RCE).

Below are the important languages and their dangerous functions.

### 1\. Python

High-risk functions

| Function             | Purpose                                                  |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| os.system()          | Executes a system command                                |
| os.popen()           | Executes a command and reads output                      |
| subprocess.call()    | Runs an external command                                 |
| subprocess.run()     | Runs an external command                                 |
| subprocess.Popen()   | Creates a subprocess                                     |
| commands.getoutput() | Executes a command and captures output (legacy Python 2) |

Example:

```python
import subprocess

subprocess.run(["whoami"])
```

### 2\. PHP

| Function     | Purpose                                           |
